{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"numbers","title":"Numbers, Math & BigInt","group":"Types, Values & Operators","order":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">One <code>number</code> type for integers and decimals — a 64-bit IEEE 754 double — plus <code>BigInt</code> for arbitrarily large integers.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Floating-point reality</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(0.1 + 0.2);\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3);\nconsole.log(Math.abs(0.1 + 0.2 - 0.3) &lt; Number.EPSILON);\nconsole.log((0.1 + 0.2).toFixed(2), Math.round(1.005 * 100) / 100);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0.30000000000000004\nfalse\ntrue\n0.30 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Money rule</div><p>0.1 has no exact binary representation (like 1/3 in decimal). Compare floats with a tolerance, and do money in <strong>integer cents</strong> (or <code>BigInt</code>, or a decimal library).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-infinity\"></i>Special values</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(1 / 0, -1 / 0, 0 / 0);\nconsole.log(typeof NaN, NaN === NaN);\nconsole.log(isNaN(\"abc\"), Number.isNaN(\"abc\"));\nconsole.log(Number.isFinite(\"5\"), isFinite(\"5\"));\nconsole.log(Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, Number.isSafeInteger(2 ** 53));\nconsole.log(2 ** 53 === 2 ** 53 + 1);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Infinity -Infinity NaN\nnumber false\ntrue false\nfalse true\n9007199254740991 false\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Global (coerces)</th><th><code>Number.*</code> (no coercion) — prefer these</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>isNaN(\"abc\")</code> → <code>true</code></td><td><code>Number.isNaN(\"abc\")</code> → <code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>isFinite(\"5\")</code> → <code>true</code></td><td><code>Number.isFinite(\"5\")</code> → <code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>parseInt</code>, <code>parseFloat</code></td><td><code>Number.parseInt</code>, <code>Number.parseFloat</code> (identical)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-123\"></i>Formatting and parsing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const n = 1234.5678;\nconsole.log(n.toFixed(2), n.toPrecision(3), (255).toString(16), (5).toString(2));\nconsole.log(Number(\"1_000\"), 1_000_000, 0xff, 0b1010, 0o17, 1e3);\nconsole.log((1234567.891).toLocaleString(\"en-US\"));\nconsole.log((1234567.891).toLocaleString(\"de-DE\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1234.57 1.23e+3 ff 101\nNaN 1000000 255 10 15 1000\n1,234,567.891\n1.234.567,891</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-function\"></i>The <code>Math</code> object</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Math.round(2.5)</code>, <code>Math.round(-2.5)</code></td><td><code>3</code>, <code>-2</code> (rounds half toward +∞)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Math.floor(-1.5)</code>, <code>Math.ceil(-1.5)</code>, <code>Math.trunc(-1.5)</code></td><td><code>-2</code>, <code>-1</code>, <code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Math.max(...arr)</code>, <code>Math.min()</code></td><td>largest; <code>Math.min()</code> with no args is <code>Infinity</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Math.abs</code>, <code>Math.sign</code>, <code>Math.sqrt</code>, <code>Math.cbrt</code>, <code>Math.hypot</code></td><td>the usual</td></tr><tr><td><code>Math.random()</code></td><td>float in <code>[0, 1)</code> — <strong>not</strong> cryptographically secure</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(Math.round(2.5), Math.round(-2.5), Math.trunc(-1.7), Math.floor(-1.7));\nconsole.log(Math.max(3, 9, 1), Math.max(...[4, 8]), Math.max(), Math.min());\nconsole.log(Math.hypot(3, 4), 2 ** 10, 7 % 3, -7 % 3);\nconst randInt = (min, max) =&gt; Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min;\nconst r = randInt(1, 6);\nconsole.log(r &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; r &lt;= 6 &amp;&amp; Number.isInteger(r));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 -2 -1 -2\n9 8 -Infinity Infinity\n5 1024 1 -1\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>%</code> is a <em>remainder</em>, not a modulo: its sign follows the dividend (<code>-7 % 3 === -1</code>). For a true modulo use <code>((n % m) + m) % m</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-numbers\"></i>BigInt</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const big = 2n ** 64n;\nconsole.log(big, typeof big);\nconsole.log(9007199254740993n + 1n, 9007199254740993 + 1);\nconsole.log(7n / 2n, BigInt(\"123\") * 2n, 5n == 5, 5n === 5);\ntry {\n  console.log(1n + 1);\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.name + \": \" + e.message);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>18446744073709551616n bigint\n9007199254740994n 9007199254740992\n3n 246n true false\nTypeError: Cannot mix BigInt and other types, use explicit conversions</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>BigInt division truncates (<code>7n / 2n === 3n</code>); no decimals.</li><li>Cannot mix with <code>number</code> in arithmetic — convert with <code>BigInt(n)</code> or <code>Number(b)</code>.</li><li>Not usable with <code>Math.*</code>; <code>JSON.stringify</code> throws on BigInt unless you add a <code>toJSON</code>.</li></ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is 0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3?</summary><div><p>Numbers are IEEE 754 binary doubles; 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be represented exactly, so their sum is <code>0.30000000000000004</code>. Compare with a tolerance (<code>Math.abs(a - b) &lt; Number.EPSILON</code>) or work in integers.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER?</summary><div><p><code>2^53 − 1</code> (9007199254740991) — the largest integer where every integer below it is exactly representable. Beyond it, <code>n + 1</code> may equal <code>n</code>; use <code>BigInt</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}